{
  "A": "Research exact integer trial division. Replace floating fmod with integer remainder, handle values below 2 correctly, reject small factors, and compare odd-only or 6k±1 traversal against division by a sieve-generated prime table. The maximum required trial divisor is 24514; use widened integer products for p*p termination rather than floating square roots. Include prime-table construction in whole-program timing, particularly for small P. Keep this tree focused on the primality kernel so its contribution is measurable. Return the fastest validated complete program, measurements, and a clearly separable primality routine.",
  "B": "Research deterministic Miller–Rabin for the certified numeric range. Bases 2, 7 and 61 suffice for all unsigned 32-bit inputs and therefore cover this contract. Handle small numbers, even numbers and bases divisible by n correctly. Widen operands to uint64_t before modular multiplication; products fit safely under the stated bound. No probabilistic acceptance or floating modular arithmetic. Compare modest small-prime predivision against immediate Miller–Rabin, including all setup and process costs. Return a validated standalone program and measurements, keeping the kernel separable for integration.",
  "C": "Research numeric dataflow and batched composite filtering. Parse fragments once and form candidates as left*10000000+right; the scale comes from the seven-character right fragment, not its numeric digit count after leading zeros disappear. Preserve the complete Cartesian product and occurrence multiplicities. Compare direct candidate construction with shared small-prime residues: combine row and column residues to reject divisible candidates, potentially using one uint32_t survivor mask per row. Preserve n==divisor if implementing a reusable filter. Apply exact primality testing to every survivor. Measure filter construction and small-P overhead; avoid large tables or threading without evidence. Keep sorting and output straightforward and byte-exact.",
  "G": "Select and integrate from measured, correctness-passing programs—not presumed additive improvements. Compare A and B standalone, then the better kernel with C's numeric front end, then optionally C's residue filtering. Retain the fastest standalone if integration regresses. Include startup, sieve/filter construction, sorting and output in unchanged official measurements; use research measurements for selection and the authorized confirmation pipeline for the final artifact, within existing budgets. Audit leading-zero scaling, widened products, array bounds, duplicate occurrences, ascending order and final newlines. Do not stop after finding a presumed matching, deduplicate results, recognize benchmark inputs, or hardcode answers. Keep compiler, target, tests, oracle and permissions unchanged; four available cores do not establish that threading will help.",
  "analysis": "The sole measured implementation is baseline-e1: correctness passed, score 0.5, mean 0.044133203414579235 seconds. There are no candidate timings or profiles. The source's likely dominant cost is floating remainder for successive integer divisors; repeated string concatenation and parsing are additional avoidable work. The certified contract limits enumeration to 900 concatenations and values to 600971707, making exact integer methods practical. A explores divisor-based primality, B explores deterministic modular-exponentiation primality, and C explores shared representation and filtering. Correctness means preserving every prime concatenation occurrence and emitting its sorted decimal value with exact formatting. Scope remains the certified two-group contract, not the contradictory example or unrestricted contest domain."
